  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Thailand: enhancing local migration health system governance in Tak province to improve migrant inclusion in universal health coverage</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">While Thailand has achieved Universal Health Coverage for Thai citizens, and different health insurance schemes that include migrants exist, challenges are still faced in the country in the area of migration health. The ongoing crisis in Myanmar has resulted in complex migration flows into the country, with more than 1.2 million migrants entering Thailand in 2024 alone. Tak province hosts the largest formal border crossing point (Myawaddy-Mae Sot). These migration flows have increased the disease burden in border areas resulting in the need to enhance coordination among health actors and strengthen migration health governance in border communities. The proposed project aims to contribute to the Government of Thailand&apos;s efforts to enhance migration health governance through an adaptable and scalable provincial approach for improved access to health services. The project will identify and remove barriers to healthcare access for migrants and increase their enrolment in health insurance schemes to achieve Universal Health Coverage (UHC). These efforts will reduce the financial burden on public hospitals in border areas. IOM will conduct this project in close coordination with the Ministry of Public Health and with Tak Provincial Health Office.</narrative>
  </description>
